{"type":"item.started","item":{"id":"item_1","type":"command_execution","command":"/bin/bash -lc 'pwd; nvidia-smi --query-gpu=name,memory.total,memory.free --format=csv 2>/dev/null || echo NO_GPU; ls -a; cat STATE.md; cat literature_review.md; cat resources.md'","aggregated_output":"","exit_code":null,"status":"in_progress"}}
{"type":"item.completed","item":{"id":"item_1","type":"command_execution","command":"/bin/bash -lc 'pwd; nvidia-smi --query-gpu=name,memory.total,memory.free --format=csv 2>/dev/null || echo NO_GPU; ls -a; cat STATE.md; cat literature_review.md; cat resources.md'","aggregated_output":"/workspaces/isolating-knowledge-updates-2d1a-codex-gpt-6-astra\nname, memory.total [MiB], memory.free [MiB]\nNVIDIA RTX A6000, 49140 MiB, 48541 MiB\n.\n..\n.claude\n.codex\n.gemini\n.git\n.gitignore\n.neurico\n.resource_finder_complete\n.venv\nREADME.md\nSTATE.md\nartifacts\ncode\ndatasets\nliterature_review.md\nlogs\nnotes\npaper_search.json\npaper_search_results\npapers\nplanning.md\npyproject.toml\nresources.md\nresults\nscripts\nuv.lock\n# Research State\n\n- Current phase: `experiment_runner`\n- Pipeline completed: `False`\n\n## Previous phases\n\nresource_finder (succeeded)\n\n## Current phase context\n\n- Phase: `experiment_runner`\n- Status: `in_progress`\n- Started: `2026-10-04T08:57:51.271437Z`\n- Next steps:\n  - Validate the report and experimental artifacts before finalizing.\n\n## Workspace check\n\n- Root: `/workspaces/isolating-knowledge-updates-2d1a-codex-gpt-6-astra`\n- Directory usable: `True`\n\n## Output validation\n\nNo phase output validation recorded yet.\n\n## Agent notes\n\n<!-- NEURICO_AGENT_NOTES_START -->\n### resource_finder\n<!-- NEURICO_AGENT_NOTES_START:resource_finder -->\nResource-finder Phases 1–5 complete. Deliverables: literature_review.md (concise synthesis), planning.md (exactly 3 ranked directions plus rejected options), resources.md, papers/README.md, datasets/README.md, code/README.md, reproducibility scripts and fresh .venv/uv.lock. Acquired 40 original PDFs, all 3 specified web articles, 4 downloaded datasets plus 1 generated arithmetic suite, and 6 pinned official repositories. All user-specified resources are local. Full chunk reading covered 8 specified papers plus Shattering; notes/chunk_reading_log.md records evidence. Linked Believe paper received main-text review only.\nData: CounterFact 21,919; GSM8K test 1,319; WikiText-2 all 3 splits; RippleEdits release 4,755 (different from paper counts); arithmetic 2,399 (405 train/115 dev/1,879 test), with explicit roles and ordinary/counterfactual labels. Raw data and cloned repositories are Git-ignored; small samples/manifests/docs are visible. notes/resource_validation.json records passing PDF/data/hash/split/code-commit checks. Completion marker: .resource_finder_complete.\nNext phase: experiment_runner. Read planning.md and datasets/README.md; check hardware/model access and baseline arithmetic competence, freeze model/tokenizer/prompts, cache base outputs, then execute only (1) preservation-constrained parametric edits, (2) string/semantic routing controls, (3) matched-token training breadth/adoption-depth comparisons. Keep semantic propagation separate from unrelated leakage; use dev selection and held-out tests.\nUnresolved limits: paper-finder 233 candidates yielded 37 locally available entries and 196 supplementary access failures after retries (notes/search_access.csv); CounterFact data license unspecified; no model weights/covariance matrices/large SDF corpora or paid API runs; upstream CUDA/dependency compatibility untested. The custom suite has correlated templates and small semantic strata. No experiments performed and no universal isolation or genuine-belief claim established. No direction-ranking drift.\n<!-- NEURICO_AGENT_NOTES_END:resource_finder -->\n\n### experiment_runner\n<!-- NEURICO_AGENT_NOTES_START:experiment_runner -->\nUpdate this section at the end of the `experiment_runner` phase.\n<!-- NEURICO_AGENT_NOTES_END:experiment_runner -->\n\n<!-- NEURICO_AGENT_NOTES_END -->\n# Literature review: Isolating knowledge updates?\n\n## Research area and central distinction\n\nCan a pretrained LLM learn `2+2=5` while preserving everything else? The literature supports measuring a tradeoff, not assuming either perfect isolation or inevitable global corruption. Knowledge-editing benchmarks usually distinguish efficacy, paraphrase generalization and locality; ripple and belief studies add downstream implications and robustness. Arithmetic work shows shared computational representations, making entity-relation editing results informative but insufficient.\n\nTwo objectives must be reported separately. **Literal exception isolation** changes only the exact input. **Semantic updating** changes equivalent questions and specified consequences while preserving unrelated behavior. A semantic edit cannot simultaneously propagate to those consequences and leave them unchanged. Moreover, ordinary arithmetic plus `2+2=5` is inconsistent: our downstream labels use an explicitly defined local rewrite semantics. Finite tests cannot establish preservation for all possible prompts.\n\n## Key evidence\n\n**Cohen, Biran, Yoran, Globerson and Geva (2023 preprint; TACL 2024), [Evaluating the Ripple Effects of Knowledge Editing](https://arxiv.org/abs/2307.12976).** RippleEdits builds Wikidata-based edits and six conditional criteria: logical generalization, two composition types, subject aliases, preservation and relation specificity. ROME/MEMIT/MEND often fail to propagate consistently; an in-context editing baseline is generally stronger. Main scores condition on successful edits and pre-known queries, so denominators matter. The abstract says5K, the inspected paper table totals4K, and the pinned public release actually contains4,755 records; use release counts. Restriction to local relationships leaves distant behavior untested. [Official code](https://github.com/edenbiran/RippleEdits) is cloned. Transfer its paired dependency/preservation design, not its factual templates, to arithmetic.\n\n**Qin, Zhang, Li, Yu and Ji (2024), [Why Does New Knowledge Create Messy Ripple Effects?](https://arxiv.org/abs/2407.12828).** GradSim measures cosine similarity between gradients of original and related answer probabilities. Experiments use RippleEdits, additional MQuAKE cases, GPT2-XL/Llama, ROME/MEMIT and supplementary editing baselines; outcomes include sampled answers and probability gains. Similarity correlates with successful propagation but also explains cases involving negation, over-ripple and multilingual failure. This is correlational; preservation tasks do not show the same relationship. Shared answer tokens can confound arithmetic interpretations. [Code](https://github.com/JiaxinQin0814/Ripple_Effect_Analysis) is linked, not cloned.\n\n**Gupta, Rao and Anumanchipalli (2024), [Model Editing at Scale Leads to Gradual and Catastrophic Forgetting](https://arxiv.org/abs/2401.07453).** Sequential CounterFact edits with ROME/MEMIT, plus FT-C/MEND comparisons, track old edits, neighborhood preference and small downstream classification sets on GPT2-XL/GPT-J. Both gradual forgetting and abrupt disabling edits occur; MEMIT delays some failures. Some individual edits can damage a fresh model, but sequential collapse is not evidence that every single edit must leak. Implementation fixes such as r-ROME make version control essential. No claim of full general-capability preservation follows from a small downstream sample.\n\n**Gangadhar and Stratos (2024), [Model Editing by Standard Fine-Tuning](https://arxiv.org/abs/2402.11078).** Conditional target loss, augmented paraphrases and replay of unedited facts make ordinary FT competitive on CounterFact, zSRE and WikiRecent. GPT-J mass-edit score86.5 versus MEMIT85.8 contrasts with single-edit GPT2-XL score83.1 versus ROME89.2; settings must not be pooled. Generation fluency/consistency can deteriorate despite strong editing scores; unrelated Wikipedia LM loss helps. Layer restriction affects locality. [Official implementation](https://github.com/au-revoir/model-editing-ft), including single-edit code, is cloned. This is the primary actionable training baseline; its score is not a universal isolation measure.\n\n**Zhang et al. (2024), [A Comprehensive Study of Knowledge Editing for LLMs](https://arxiv.org/abs/2401.01286).** KnowEdit combines six datasets and compares external-memory, added-representation and parameter-update approaches across reliability, generalization, locality and portability. Fine-tuning can be strong; portability remains difficult. General-task checks using a few edits do not conflict with later sequential collapse. Parameter sparsity is not behavioral sparsity. [EasyEdit](https://github.com/zjunlp/EasyEdit) is an available broader implementation, not cloned because narrower official repositories cover the chosen methods.\n\n**Nikankin, Reusch, Mueller and Belinkov (2024 preprint; ICLR 2025), [Arithmetic Without Algorithms](https://arxiv.org/abs/2410.21272).** Activation interventions and neuron analyses across arithmetic tasks find overlapping MLP heuristics for ranges, modular patterns and output values. On the studied bounded addition task Llama3-8B is much stronger than GPT-J; baseline competence is model/task dependent. Causal ablation supports a shared heuristic mechanism, but the single-token/range restrictions limit extrapolation and no isolated false-fact training study is supplied. [Code](https://github.com/technion-cs-nlp/llm-arithmetic-heuristics) is linked. Include neighboring sums, other operators and answer4/5 controls.\n\n**Kantamneni and Tegmark (2025), [Language Models Use Trigonometry to Do Addition](https://arxiv.org/abs/2502.00873).** Linear-plus-Fourier helical representations, activation patching and neuron analyses are evaluated on10,000 additions with operands0–99 in GPT-J, Pythia and Llama3.1. Reported accuracies differ substantially (80.5%,77.2%,98% in their setup). Periodic structure and ±10 mistakes motivate carry/modular and distance-stratified tests. These geometric descriptions can coexist with heuristic neurons. The study does not prove that modifying one addition requires changing all related answers. Implementation links are preserved in the archived paper; no separate implementation was tested.\n\n**Wei Zhang et al. (ICML 2024), [Interpreting and Improving LLMs in Arithmetic Calculation](https://arxiv.org/abs/2409.01659).** Causal localization in Llama2 separates operand/operator-sensitive attention and calculation-associated middle MLPs. Selective tuning of32 attention heads improves arithmetic with less general-task loss than full FT in reported comparisons, including MMLU/CSQA checks. This teaches correct arithmetic rather than one counterfactual, so it motivates a restricted-parameter comparator without demonstrating isolated editing. Full methodology/appendices were read; no implementation was cloned.\n\n**Wang et al. (2025), [Modifying LLM Beliefs with Synthetic Document Finetuning](https://alignment.anthropic.com/2025/modifying-beliefs-via-sdf/).** Diverse documents describing a counterfactual world produce broader downstream adoption than merely asserting a fact. Open-model experiments include LoRA and full FT; document generation, belief questions and unrelated capability tests matter. The June20 correction reduces an apparent benefit after a context-generation bug was fixed. Plausibility, truth-versus-generation tests and capability changes qualify simple “belief implantation” claims. [SDF code](https://github.com/safety-research/false-facts) is cloned. Match training tokens and replay, not only numbers of documents.\n\n**Slocum et al. (2025), [Believe It or Not](https://alignment.anthropic.com/2025/believe-it-or-not/), [linked paper](https://arxiv.org/abs/2510.17941).** Compares optimized prompting, AlphaEdit and SDF over24 facts using generality, robustness and internal representations. SDF training uses40K synthetic documents plus40K C4 examples with document-tag conditioning in the main setup. Prompting and SDF generalize, while SDF is generally more robust; implausible facts can remain detectable through adversarial probing. These are operational belief dimensions, not a binary truth test. [Code](https://github.com/safety-research/believe-it-or-not) is cloned. The specified blog was read fully; the90-page linked paper's main text pp1–12 was reviewed, with appendices archived rather than claimed fully read.\n\n**David Vella Zarb (2025), [What Happens When You Train Models on False Facts?](https://www.lesswrong.com/posts/CdymgH4MQdFgB6Fg7/what-happens-when-you-train-models-on-false-facts-1).** Exploratory Llama3B/8B experiments use three false facts,40K/60K/80K synthetic documents, downstream/negated/control questions and probes. Probe separability changes also occur under unrelated math-training domain shift; example-count-matched controls need not be token matched. Small question sets and probe calibration limit inference. Useful controls, lower evidential weight than a controlled replication. Full post archived; no separate code tested.\n\n**Nishi et al. (2024 preprint; ICML 2025), [Representation Shattering](https://arxiv.org/abs/2410.17194).** Highest-ranked paper-finder result. A small transformer learns cyclic graph relations over2,048 entities; ROME, MEMIT, PMET and AlphaEdit edits damage direct recall and logical/compositional inference. Representation distortion correlates with loss (reported r²=.905); greater counterfactual graph distance tends to worsen both. Calendar and city-country examples extend the phenomenon to pretrained models. Isomap preprocessing, heterogeneous edit outcomes, and limited naturalistic cases prevent a universal impossibility conclusion. [Code](https://github.com/KentoNishi/KE-ICML-2025) is available; this direction is pruned from implementation because training a toy model changes the target question.\n\n## Common methods, baselines and metrics\n\nROME ([Meng et al.,2022](https://arxiv.org/abs/2202.05262)) supplies a rank-one MLP-update baseline and CounterFact; AlphaEdit ([Fang et al.,2024/2025](https://arxiv.org/abs/2410.02355)) adds null-space preservation relative to sampled keys. Those constraints are not guarantees over all prompts. SERAC ([Mitchell et al.,2022](https://arxiv.org/abs/2206.06520)) routes between memory-conditioned editing and a base model, making the scope classifier part of the locality problem. These supplementary papers were screened rather than exhaustively read.\n\nCompare unchanged model, sham/true-fact FT, masked FT/LoRA, preservation-regularized FT and exact-string routing. Add canonicalized routing and an in-context counterfactual condition to distinguish string memorization from semantic scope. ROME/AlphaEdit are optional comparators within the parametric direction; model-specific arithmetic subject representation must be justified.\n\nMeasure exact edit success and target probability; held-out paraphrase transfer; answer agreement and accuracy separately on neighboring/distant arithmetic and other operations; desired downstream propagation versus matched controls; false-trigger rate; unrelated factual/math capability; and full-vocabulary KL/NLL on identical held-out text prefixes. Old-vs-new target preference can hide distribution changes. Report success-conditioned and unconditional results, base-known counts, global answer5 bias and uncertainty clustered by template family. Preserve the base model's errors when measuring agreement, while reporting correctness independently.\n\n## Data and recommendations\n\nCounterFact supplies factual locality; RippleEdits supplies conditional downstream structure; GSM8K test measures broad math capability; WikiText-2 train/dev/test supports replay and held-out language modeling. None establishes universal unrelatedness. The generated arithmetic suite adds exact/paraphrase/near/far/operator/dependent strata with disjoint splits and a documented intentional exact-target overlap. It is a pilot benchmark needing baseline and semantic audit, not an established dataset.\n\nThe top three directions are fixed in [planning.md](planning.md): (1) parametric efficacy–locality frontiers under preservation constraints; (2) exact versus semantic exception boundaries; (3) training breadth versus downstream/robust adoption. Start with a small accessible competent model, then a second model if resources permit. Select hyperparameters on dev and compare matched edit efficacy. A zero-leakage finite test result supports a bounded claim only. A routing guarantee is architectural noninterference outside its predicate; it does not answer whether training changed what the model treats as true.\n\n## Review provenance\n\nPaper-finder diligent search returned233 candidates;37 are locally available after reconciliation,196 remain unavailable after alternate-source retries. Forty distinct PDFs were acquired in total, plus all three specified web articles. All64 chunks of the eight specified PDFs and all10 Shattering chunks were visually reviewed; detailed contemporaneous notes: [chunk log](notes/chunk_reading_log.md). Other papers received title/abstract screening, with methods checked only where explicitly noted. [Paper catalog](papers/README.md), [access ledger](notes/search_access.csv) and [resource catalog](resources.md) distinguish acquired, screened and deeply read sources. No model experiments were performed.\n# Resources catalog\n\n## Summary\n\n40 original paper PDFs, 3 specified web articles, 4 downloaded datasets plus 1 generated arithmetic suite, and 6 official code repositories. All explicitly supplied sources are local. Supplemental search coverage is incomplete: 37 of 233 paper-finder entries are available after title reconciliation; 196 downloads remain unavailable after retries. This is a resource handoff, not completed model experiments.\n\n## Papers\n\n| Title | Authors | Year | Local file | Key information |\n|---|---|---:|---|---|\n| [Editing Factual Knowledge in Language Models](https://arxiv.org/pdf/2104.08164v2) | Nicola De Cao, Wilker Aziz, Ivan Titov | 2021 | [2104.08164.pdf](papers/2104.08164.pdf) | Learned factual editor; historical baseline. |\n| [Locating and Editing Factual Associations in GPT](https://arxiv.org/pdf/2202.05262v5) | Meng, Kevin et al. | 2022 | [2202.05262.pdf](papers/2202.05262.pdf) | ROME and CounterFact foundation; parametric comparator. |\n| [Memory-Based Model Editing at Scale](https://arxiv.org/pdf/2206.06520v1) | Mitchell, Eric et al. | 2022 | [2206.06520.pdf](papers/2206.06520.pdf) | SERAC memory/routing; exception-scope control. |\n| [Propagating Knowledge Updates to LMs Through Distillation](https://arxiv.org/pdf/2306.09306) | Shankar Padmanabhan, Yasumasa Onoe, Michael J.Q. Zhang, Greg Durrett, … | 2023 | [2306.09306.pdf](papers/2306.09306.pdf) | Distillation-based propagation; archival alternative. |\n| [Evaluating the Ripple Effects of Knowledge Editing in Language Models](https://arxiv.org/abs/2307.12976) | Cohen, Roi et al. | 2023 | [2307.12976.pdf](papers/2307.12976.pdf) | Desired ripple versus preserved facts; core evaluation design. |\n| [PMET: Precise Model Editing in a Transformer](https://arxiv.org/pdf/2308.08742v6) | Xiaopeng Li, Shasha Li, Shezheng Song, Jing Yang, Jun Ma, Jie Yu | 2023 | [2308.08742.pdf](papers/2308.08742.pdf) | PMET parameter update method; archival comparator. |\n| [Evaluating the Reversal Curse in Model Editing](https://arxiv.org/pdf/2310.10322v4) | Hao-Yang Xu, Jun-Yu Ma, Zhen-Hua Ling, Quan Liu, Cong Liu, Jia-Chen Gu | 2023 | [2310.10322.pdf](papers/2310.10322.pdf) | Reverse-relation transfer can fail; robustness motivation. |\n| [Knowledge Editing for Large Language Models: A Survey](https://arxiv.org/pdf/2310.16218v4) | Song Wang, Yao-Chen Zhu, Hao-Chen Liu, Zai-Yi Zheng, Chen Chen, Jun-Do… | 2023 | [2310.16218.pdf](papers/2310.16218.pdf) | Method taxonomy; contextual survey. |\n| [A Comprehensive Study of Knowledge Editing for Large Language Models](https://arxiv.org/abs/2401.01286) | Zhang, Ningyu et al. | 2024 | [2401.01286.pdf](papers/2401.01286.pdf) | KnowEdit benchmark and broad editor comparison. |\n| [Model Editing Harms General Abilities of Large Language Models: