ang, Kai-Lin Jiang, Xiaobo Wang, Jiaqi Li, Yi-Fa… | 2024 | [2406.11194.pdf](papers/2406.11194.pdf) | Self-induced distribution editing; not the same ICE as prompt baseline. |\n| [Why Does New Knowledge Create Messy Ripple Effects in LLMs?](https://arxiv.org/abs/2407.12828) | Qin, Jiaxin et al. | 2024 | [2407.12828.pdf](papers/2407.12828.pdf) | GradSim correlation with ripple, negation and over-ripple. |\n| [Resolving Lexical Bias in Model Editing](https://arxiv.org/pdf/2408.10411v3) | Hammad Rizwan, Domenic Rosati, Ga Wu, Hassan Sajjad | 2024 | [2408.10411.pdf](papers/2408.10411.pdf) | Lexical shortcuts; held-out wording motivation. |\n| [Interpreting and Improving Large Language Models in Arithmetic Calculation](https://arxiv.org/abs/2409.01659) | Zhang, Wei et al. | 2024 | [2409.01659.pdf](papers/2409.01659.pdf) | Selective arithmetic tuning; not counterfactual isolation. |\n| [AlphaEdit: Null-Space Constrained Knowledge Editing for Language Models](https://arxiv.org/abs/2410.02355) | Fang, Junfeng et al. | 2024 | [2410.02355.pdf](papers/2410.02355.pdf) | Null-space preservation; optional parametric comparator. |\n| [Representation Shattering in Transformers: A Synthetic Study with Knowledge Editing](https://arxiv.org/pdf/2410.17194v5) | Kento Nishi, Maya Okawa, Rahul Ramesh, Mikail Khona, E. Lubana, Hideno… | 2024 | [2410.17194.pdf](papers/2410.17194.pdf) | Representation shattering; top search result, fully read. |\n| [Arithmetic Without Algorithms: Language Models Solve Math With a Bag of Heuristics](https://arxiv.org/abs/2410.21272) | Nikankin, Yaniv et al. | 2024 | [2410.21272.pdf](papers/2410.21272.pdf) | Shared arithmetic heuristics; neighboring/operator controls. |\n| [Joint Knowledge Editing for Information Enrichment and Probability Promotion](https://arxiv.org/pdf/2412.17872v1) | Wenhang Shi, Yiren Chen, Shuqing Bian, Xinyi Zhang, Zhe Zhao, Pengfei … | 2024 | [2412.17872.pdf](papers/2412.17872.pdf) | Information enrichment and probability promotion; archive. |\n| [Language Models Use Trigonometry to Do Addition](https://arxiv.org/abs/2502.00873) | Kantamneni, Subhash et al. | 2025 | [2502.00873.pdf](papers/2502.00873.pdf) | Helical addition representations; modular diagnostics. |\n| [CoME: An Unlearning-based Approach to Conflict-free Model Editing](https://arxiv.org/pdf/2502.15826) | Dahyun Jung, Jaehyung Seo, Jaewook Lee, Chanjun Park, Heu-Jeoung Lim | 2025 | [2502.15826.pdf](papers/2502.15826.pdf) | Conflicting edits and unlearning; archival alternative. |\n| [Knowledge Updating? No More Model Editing! Just Selective Contextual Reasoning](https://arxiv.org/pdf/2503.05212v1) | Guoxiu He, Xin Song, Aixin Sun | 2025 | [2503.05212.pdf](papers/2503.05212.pdf) | Selective contextual reasoning; routing context. |\n| [EAMET: Robust Massive Model Editing via Embedding Alignment Optimization](https://arxiv.org/pdf/2505.11876v4) | Yanbo Dai, Zhenlan Ji, Zong-Jie Li, Shuai Wang | 2025 | [2505.11876.pdf](papers/2505.11876.pdf) | Mass editing alignment; pruned scale direction. |\n| [REACT: Representation Extraction And Controllable Tuning to Overcome Overfitting in LLM Knowledge Editing](https://arxiv.org/pdf/2505.18933v1) | Haitian Zhong, Yuhuan Liu, Zi-Yang Xu, Guofan Liu, Q. Liu, Shu Wu, Zhe… | 2025 | [2505.18933.pdf](papers/2505.18933.pdf) | Controllable tuning and overfitting; archival context. |\n| [Towards a Principled Evaluation of Knowledge Editors](https://arxiv.org/pdf/2507.05937v1) | Sebastian Pohl, Max Ploner, Alan Akbik | 2025 | [2507.05937.pdf](papers/2507.05937.pdf) | Evaluation methodology; interpret scores cautiously. |\n| [MedMKEB: A Comprehensive Knowledge Editing Benchmark for Medical Multimodal Large Language Models](https://arxiv.org/pdf/2508.05083) | Dexuan Xu, Jie-Yi Wang, Zhongyan Chai, Yongzhi Cao, Han-Ping Wang, Hua… | 2025 | [2508.05083.pdf](papers/2508.05083.pdf) | Medical multimodal benchmark; out of arithmetic scope. |\n| [A Dual-Axis Taxonomy of Knowledge Editing for LLMs: From Mechanisms to Functions](https://arxiv.org/pdf/2508.08795) | Amir Mohammad Salehoof, Ali Ramezani, Yadollah Yaghoobzadeh, M. N. Ahm… | 2025 | [2508.08795.pdf](papers/2508.08795.pdf) | Taxonomy; broad archival survey. |\n| [Towards Meta-Cognitive Knowledge Editing for Multimodal LLMs](https://arxiv.org/pdf/2509.05714v1) | Zhao-Yu Fan, Kai-Hang Pan, Mingze Zhou, Bosheng Qin, Jun-Cheng Li, She… | 2025 | [2509.05714.pdf](papers/2509.05714.pdf) | Multimodal metacognitive editing; out of scope. |\n| [Diagnosing Model Editing via Knowledge Spectrum](https://arxiv.org/pdf/2509.17482) | T. Pan, Chung-Chi Chen, Hen-Hsen Huang, Hsin-Hsi Chen | 2025 | [2509.17482.pdf](papers/2509.17482.pdf) | Knowledge-spectrum diagnostic; supplementary screening. |\n| [Believe It or Not: How Deeply do LLMs Believe Implanted Facts?](https://arxiv.org/abs/2510.17941) | Slocum, Stewart et al. | 2025 | [2510.17941.pdf](papers/2510.17941.pdf) | Depth of implanted beliefs; main text only plus full blog. |\n| [Layer of Truth: Probing Belief Shifts under Continual Pre-Training Poisoning](https://arxiv.org/pdf/2510.26829v3) | S. Churina, Niranjan Chebrolu, Kokil Jaidka | 2025 | [2510.26829.pdf](papers/2510.26829.pdf) | Belief-shift probes; diagnostics not causal proof. |\n| [An information-theoretic framework for robust large language model editing](https://arxiv.org/pdf/2512.16227) | Qizhou Chen, Chengyu Wang, Tao-Lin Zhang, Xiao-Feng He | 2025 | [2512.16227.pdf](papers/2512.16227.pdf) | Information-theoretic framing; screened only. |\n| [The Model Agreed, But Didn't Learn: Diagnosing Surface Compliance in Large Language Models](https://arxiv.org/pdf/2604.05995v1) | Xiaojie Gu, Zi Huang, W. Hong, Jian Xie, Renze Lou, Kai Zhang | 2026 | [2604.05995.pdf](papers/2604.05995.pdf) | Surface compliance versus adoption; screened only. |\n| [ManiEdit: Sequential Unstructured Knowledge Editing for Language Models from a Manifold Perspective](https://arxiv.org/pdf/2609.33534) | Rui Liu, Chen-Heng Zhang, Hao-Xuan Li, Zhou-Chen Lin | 2026 | [2609.33534.pdf](papers/2609.33534.pdf) | Sequential manifold editing; screened, outside chosen scope. |\n| [SSS: Editing Factual Knowledge in Language Models towards Semantic Sparse Space](https://aclanthology.org/2024.findings-acl.331.pdf) | Hua-Zheng Wang, Hai-Feng Sun, Jing-Yu Wang, Qi Qi, Zi-Xuan Xia, Mengha… | 2024 | [search_124.pdf](papers/search_124.pdf) | Semantic-sparsity regularization; abstract read from PDF. |\n\nFull author lists, reading status and PDF links: [papers/README.md](papers/README.md). Version bytes/pages/SHA256: [notes/papers_manifest.json](notes/papers_manifest.json). Years primarily indicate first submission; conference/revision years can differ. Chunk PDFs are not counted as separate papers.\n\n| Specified web article | Local archive | Use |\n|---|---|---|\n| [Modifying beliefs via SDF](https://alignment.anthropic.com/2025/modifying-beliefs-via-sdf/) | notes/sdf.html and .txt | Document diversity, controls, June2025 correction |\n| [Believe It or Not](https://alignment.anthropic.com/2025/believe-it-or-not/) | notes/believe.html and .txt | Generality/robustness/representations |\n| [False-facts experiments](https://www.lesswrong.com/posts/CdymgH4MQdFgB6Fg7/what-happens-when-you-train-models-on-false-facts-1) | notes/false_facts.html and .txt | Probe confounds; exploratory evidence |\n\n## Data\n\n| Resource | Source and local path | Size / splits | Role |\n|---|---|---|---|\n| CounterFact | [azhx/counterfact](https://huggingface.co/datasets/azhx/counterfact); datasets/counterfact/ | 21,919 records; train19,728/test2,191;12.39MB | Mandated factual locality control |\n| GSM8K | [official source](https://github.com/openai/grade-school-math); datasets/gsm8k/ | Test1,319;0.75MB JSONL | Math capability, not uniformly unrelated |\n| WikiText-2 raw | [Salesforce/wikitext](https://huggingface.co/datasets/Salesforce/wikitext); datasets/wikitext2/ | Train36,718/dev3,760/test4,358;7.75MB parquet | Replay, NLL and KL controls |\n| RippleEdits | [official release](https://github.com/edenbiran/RippleEdits); datasets/ripple_edits/ | Popular885/random1,922/recent1,948 =4,755;42.40MB | Conditional dependency/preservation design |\n| Arithmetic isolation (generated) | scripts/build_arithmetic_suite.py; datasets/arithmetic_isolation/ | Train405/dev115/test1,879 =2,399;0.74MB JSONL | Main hypothesis-specific pilot suite |\n\nAll row counts are locally measured. Blank WikiText rows are retained and counted separately. Schemas, provenance, licenses, loading and exact download commands: [datasets/README.md](datasets/README.md). Small actual samples are tracked; raw data are Git-ignored. CounterFact license is unspecified in the hosted card. Arithmetic suite semantics are explicit and not a globally coherent replacement for arithmetic.\n\n## Code\n\n| Repository | Pinned commit | Local path | Purpose |\n|---|---|---|---|\n| [model-editing-ft](https://github.com/au-revoir/model-editing-ft) | `94e4ce075ee564f20e07cc22294207ac2b1a94c9` | code/model-editing-ft/ | Single-edit loss/trainer and replay |\n| [RippleEdits](https://github.com/edenbiran/RippleEdits) | `54f3b88af4895a3aacb580ec63ce7ae857185040` | code/RippleEdits/ | Conditional ripple evaluation and benchmark |\n| [rome](https://github.com/kmeng01/rome) | `0874014cd9837e4365f3e6f3c71400ef11509e04` | code/rome/ | Rank-one baseline and CounterFact evaluator |\n| [AlphaEdit](https://github.com/jianghoucheng/AlphaEdit) | `b84624f44dfe8fc6cd9e41df916c44124a0c46dc` | code/AlphaEdit/ | Null-space preservation comparator |\n| [false-facts](https://github.com/safety-research/false-facts) | `4d6e6c4807fc07816f3d29a7f336c944bd8f31df` | code/false-facts/ | Original SDF data/training/evaluation |\n| [believe-it-or-not](https://github.com/safety-research/believe-it-or-not) | `b22a45a8c53254b9278e409f5ffef4349a039199` | code/believe-it-or-not/ | Generality, robustness and belief-depth pipeline |\n\nReadmes/dependencies/entrypoints inspected; no heavy packages, models, submodules or paid APIs installed/run. [code/README.md](code/README.md) describes version conflicts, GPU requirements, licenses and adaptation points. Clones preserve upstream embedded files; experiment-ready datasets are separated under datasets/.\n\n## Search and selection\n\nPaper-finder diligent search was used first, after installing its missing httpx dependency in the fresh uv environment. Search terms covered knowledge-editing locality, counterfactual arithmetic, ripple effects, belief adoption, SDF and arithmetic mechanisms. Full output: paper_search.json; per-attempt results: notes/search_downloads.jsonl and notes/search_downloads_retry.jsonl. ArXiv/official paper pages, Semantic Scholar, GitHub and Hugging Face supplemented the search; broad web queries also checked Scholar/Papers with Code. No private dataset or Kaggle credential was needed.\n\nEvery returned candidate received a download attempt; failed Semantic Scholar retrievals were retried through arXiv title matching. Rate limiting (429), gateway/API failures and unmatched titles prevented196 supplementary entries from resolving to local PDFs. [notes/search_access.csv](notes/search_access.csv) reconciles failures against PDFs already downloaded independently. Missing abstracts were screened by title; the acquired SSS abstract was read from its first PDF page. This is a structured resource search, not a registered systematic review or exhaustive current-SOTA claim.\n\nAll eight user-specified arXiv PDFs were deep-read using three-page chunks; all chunks, including appendices, were visually inspected. The highest-ranked Shattering paper also received complete chunk review. The three specified web articles were fully read. The linked90-page Believe paper was main-text reviewed only. [notes/chunk_reading_log.md](notes/chunk_reading_log.md) preserves immediate notes. Broader downloaded papers were screened, not uniformly deep-read.\n\n## Experiment handoff\n\nThe evidence-weighted ranking and rejected directions are fixed in [planning.md](planning.md): (1) preservation-constrained parametric editing; (2) exact and semantic routing boundaries; (3) training breadth versus depth of adoption. These share one frozen evaluation suite. Additional papers do not expand the implementation search space.\n\nStart by checking model/tokenizer revisions, hardware/access and base arithmetic competence; then cache base outputs/logits. Adapt the single-edit FT loss first, include unchanged/sham/true-fact controls, and compare methods at matched efficacy using dev-selected hyperparameters. Keep exact-string routing labeled as a wrapper control. Use repeated seeds and family-aware uncertainty. Report desired semantic propagation separately from harmful preservation failures.\n\nPrimary measures: exact efficacy, paraphrase transfer, near/far/operator agreement and accuracy, paired downstream ordinary/edited-rule agreement, unrelated factual/math behavior, full-distribution KL and held-out NLL, answer5 bias, parameter change and latency. Numeric AST oracles and lexical samples are ready; broad SDF corpora are intentionally not generated in resource gathering and require a matched-token generation protocol in direction3.\n\n## Reproducibility and limitations\n\nFresh .venv, pyproject.toml and uv.lock isolate resource dependencies. Run `uv sync`, followed by dataset/repository scripts documented above. No conda or ambient research environment was used. All original PDFs parse; data hashes/row counts and git exclusions are verified in notes/resource_validation.json. Model weights, covariance matrices and large synthetic-document corpora are not downloaded; full published-scale replications therefore remain outside the ready-to-run resource set. The generated benchmark is deterministic but small/templated in semantic strata. Neither zero observed leakage nor a changed probe establishes universal isolation or genuine belief.\n\nPipeline state is preserved in STATE.md; only the resource_finder-owned notes are updated. Completion is signaled by .resource_finder_complete after final validation.\n","exit_code":0,"status":"completed"}}
{"type":"item.started","item":{"id":"item_2","type":"command_execution","command":"/bin/bash -lc \"pwd; rg --files -g AGENTS.md -g '\"'!code/**'\"' -g '\"'!papers/**'\"'; cat planning.md; cat pyproject.toml; cat datasets/README.md; cat code/README.md; cat /workspaces/isolating-knowledge-updates-2d1a-codex-gpt-6-astra/.codex/skills/data-analysis/SKILL.md\"","aggregated_output":"","exit_code":null,"status":"in_progress"}}
{"type":"item.completed","item":{"id":"item_2","type":"command_execution","command":"/bin/bash -lc \"pwd; rg --files -g AGENTS.md -g '\"'!code/**'\"' -g '\"'!papers/**'\"'; cat planning.md; cat pyproject.toml; cat datasets/README.md; cat code/README.md; cat /workspaces/isolating-knowledge-updates-2d1a-codex-gpt-6-astra/.codex/skills/data-analysis/SKILL.md\"","aggregated_output":"/workspaces/isolating-knowledge-updates-2d1a-codex-gpt-6-astra\n# Research plan: isolating a single arithmetic update\n\n## Scope and evidence\nThe question is empirical: how small can measured behavioral change be at matched edit success? No finite benchmark establishes \"nothing else changed\" for every possible input. A changed belief is operationalized by held-out semantic generalization, downstream use, and resistance to correction; these are separate measures, not a metaphysical verdict. `2+2=5` is inconsistent with ordinary arithmetic axioms. Downstream tests therefore use an explicit local evaluation rule, not a claim that all familiar algebra remains coherent.\n\nSearch phrases: knowledge editing locality; counterfactual arithmetic; ripple effects; model editing fine-tuning preservation; representation shattering; synthetic document fine-tuning beliefs; arithmetic circuits; semantic generalization versus lexical memorization. Paper-finder diligent search returned 233 candidates. All were title/abstract screened where available; downloads were attempted for every entry, with arXiv fallback. Many broad results are archival, not implementation recommendations. See paper_search.json and notes/search_downloads_retry.jsonl.\n\n## Direction ranking (fixed before experiments)\nScores are 1–5: literature support E, hypothesis relevance R, expected information gain I, feasibility F. Total = E+R+I+F; ties prefer feasibility. Scores are prospective judgments, not experimental measurements.\n\n| Rank | Direction | E | R | I | F | Total | Decision and evidence |\n|---|---|---:|---:|---:|---:|---:|---|\n| 1 | Parametric isolation frontier: masked fine-tuning with preservation constraints | 5 | 5 | 5 | 5 | 20 | KEEP. Standard FT (2402.11078) is a strong baseline; RippleEdits and forgetting results require broader locality tests. |\n| 2 | Exception boundary: exact-string and semantic routing controls versus weight edits | 4 | 5 | 5 | 5 | 19 | KEEP. SERAC and WISE motivate explicit scope/routing; lexical-bias work motivates held-out wording. A wrapper is a control, not evidence that training achieved isolation. |\n| 3 | Training breadth versus depth of adoption | 5 | 5 | 5 | 3 | 18 | KEEP. SDF and Believe It or Not distinguish generality, robustness and representations. Small matched-data study is feasible; 70B/40k-document replication is not assumed. |\n| 4 | Arithmetic neuron/helix surgery | 4 | 5 | 4 | 2 | 15 | PRUNE. Heuristics and trigonometry provide mechanisms but bespoke localization/patching adds major model-specific work before testing the central behavioral question. |\n| 5 | Probe-only diagnosis of true belief | 3 | 3 | 3 | 3 | 12 | PRUNE. Linear decodability is not causal use; domain shifts confound probes (false-facts post). |\n| 6 | Train a synthetic graph/arithmetic model from scratch | 5 | 2 | 3 | 2 | 12 | PRUNE. Shattering provides a clean mechanism, but the target is an otherwise normal pretrained LLM. |\n| 7 | Thousands of sequential edits or multimodal editing | 4 | 1 | 2 | 2 | 9 | PRUNE. Different scope; single-edit frontier comes first. |\n\nDo not add implementation directions later unless new evidence invalidates this ranking; document any revision in STATE.md. ROME/AlphaEdit are optional comparators within direction 1, not new directions. Mechanistic probes are optional diagnostics only after the behavioral result.\n\n## Concrete experiment handoff\n1. Freeze a model revision/tokenizer, chat template, precision, evaluation suite and base logits. Pilot one accessible 1–3B instruction model; require correct baseline target and adequate neighboring arithmetic before editing. Confirm target under both raw completion and native chat formatting. If inadequate, use a stronger accessible model, documenting exclusion. Do not infer editing failure from a base model that never knew the fact.\n2. Direction 1: unchanged control; answer-token masked FT; LoRA FT; preservation-regularized FT. Sweep step budget and KL/replay strength on dev only. Prefer a modest restricted layer set before expensive full-model FT. Compare at matched target success (e.g. >=95% target probability and greedy answer5); report failed edits too. ROME/AlphaEdit require meaningful subject spans and model-specific covariance statistics; arithmetic is not a plug-in CounterFact relation.\n3. Direction 2: intercept exact raw user string `2+2=` and return5; then a hand-audited canonicalized arithmetic predicate; optionally semantic retrieval gate. Measure false triggers on near misses and nontriggers on paraphrases. Record router input boundary (raw text versus entire serialized chat), coverage, latency and whether weights changed. Exact deterministic routing has guaranteed noninterference only outside its predicate and only with an otherwise unchanged deterministic execution path.\n4. Direction 3: same optimizer/update budget and total training tokens across repeated exact prompt, disjoint paraphrase training, and small coherent synthetic-document training. Include true-fact/sham fine-tune controls and equal-token unrelated replay. Generated documents require separate construction and factual/control audit; no paid generation is assumed here. The supplied train templates support the first two conditions; test templates must never become training documents.\n5. Use >=3 optimization seeds where training is stochastic, fixed decoding for primary outcomes, and optional sampled decoding with fixed seeds. Estimate uncertainty by template-family/pair bootstrap, not treating correlated variants as independent. Keep all final test prompts hidden from optimization and selection. Hash model/dataset/config artifacts.\n\n## Required outcome vector\nReport exact efficacy; held-out semantic transfer; lexical false-trigger rate; near/far arithmetic answer agreement and accuracy; downstream old-rule/new-rule agreement; unrelated answer agreement; teacher-forced full-vocabulary KL and NLL delta on held-out text; latency and parameter/update size. Compute KL on identical base reference prefixes, not each model's divergent generated continuations. Answer5 frequency on unrelated questions diagnoses global output bias. Treat GSM8K as a math capability control, not automatically independent of 2+2. CounterFact measures factual spillover and is not the main arithmetic benchmark.\n\nKeep two separate desiderata: (A) literal exception isolation, which penalizes changes everywhere except the exact key; (B) semantic update, which expects transfer to equivalent and explicitly dependent queries but penalizes unrelated changes. Plot efficacy-versus-leakage Pareto curves; do not collapse all strata into one mean. Inverse identities and adversarial correction prompts are diagnostics without a uniquely coherent global counterfactual gold. A zero observed failure count is a finite-suite statement; the iid rule-of-three bound does not apply naively to correlated templates.\n\nResource gathering does not train models. Hardware, model access, and compute budget remain to be checked by experiment_runner. Start with data/model baseline validation, then directions 1–3 in rank order.\n[project]\nname = \"research-workspace\"\nversion = \"0.1.0\"\ndescription = \"Resources for isolated counterfactual arithmetic editing\"\nrequires-python = \">=3.10\"\ndependencies = [\n    \"beautifulsoup4>=4.15.0\",\n    \"httpx>=0.28.1\",\n    \"pillow>=12.3.0\",\n    \"pyarrow>=25.0.1\",\n    \"pymupdf>=1.28.2\",\n    \"pypdf>=6.19.0\",\n    \"requests>=2.34.2\",\n]\n\n[build-system]\nrequires = [\"hatchling\"]\nbuild-backend = \"hatchling.build\"\n\n[tool.uv]\npackage = false\n# Datasets\n\nAll data are locally available. Large/raw files are excluded by this directory's `.gitignore`; samples are below100KB. Download scripts use pinned revisions. Run from the repository root in the isolated environment:\n\n```bash\npwd\nuv sync\nuv run python scripts/download_datasets.py\nuv run python scripts/download_controls.py\nuv run python scripts/build_arithmetic_suite.py\n```\n\n## CounterFact (user-specified)\n\nSource: [azhx/counterfact](https://huggingface.co/datasets/azhx/counterfact), pinned revision `c01c413f856ee38f5c080c9fc5e87aff478e2ff9`; original benchmark from ROME. Local `counterfact/*.parquet`. Train19,728 and test2,191 records (21,919 total),12,387,190 compressed bytes. Seven fields: `case_id`, `pararel_idx`, structured `requested_rewrite` (prompt,subject,relation_id,target_true,target_new), and lists of paraphrase, neighborhood, attribute and generation prompts. No top-level nulls; all rewrites nonempty. Hashes and download URLs: `validation.json`; actual three-record sample: `counterfact_sample.json`.\n\nUse to measure factual-answer spillover under the arithmetic edit and optionally sanity-check existing editors. Preserve this mirror's splits; do not assume they match every paper's evaluation partition. It does not contain arithmetic edits and cannot replace the custom suite. The hosted card does not specify a data license; provenance is known but redistribution terms remain unresolved. A code repository's license is not automatically a dataset license.\n\n## GSM8K test\n\nSource: [official grade-school-math repository](https://github.com/openai/grade-school-math), commit `3101c7d5072418e28b9008a6636bde82a006892c`. Local `gsm8k/test.jsonl`,1,319 examples. Fields `question` and `answer`; latter includes reasoning and a `####` final answer. All records parse, have nonempty questions and final-answer delimiters. Official repository MIT license saved locally in `gsm8k/LICENSE`. Only test split downloaded; never train on it. `gsm8k_sample.json` contains three actual examples.\n\nUse as math capability control, with a fixed answer parser. These problems may implicitly depend on2+2 and are not uniformly unrelated. Report overall accuracy and inspect changed items; do not score all counterfactual propagation as unrelated leakage.\n\n## WikiText-2 raw v1\n\nSource: [Salesforce/wikitext](https://huggingface.co/datasets/Salesforce/wikitext), revision `b08601e04326c79dfdd32d625aee71d232d685c3`. Local `wikitext2/{train,validation,test}.parquet`. Train36,718, validation3,760, test4,358 rows, single string field `text`. Empty lines and article headings are intentional; counts appear in `control_validation.json`. The card lists CC-BY-SA3.0 and GFDL. `wikitext2_sample.json` contains three nonblank test rows.\n\nTrain split can supply unrelated replay/preservation text; validation selects regularization/checkpoints; test is held out for NLL/KL only. Concatenate according to documented article boundaries and use identical tokenization/windows before and after editing. Drop blank rows consistently or preserve their separators; record the choice. This is a narrow text-distribution control, not proof that all unrelated behavior is preserved.\n\n## Arithmetic isolation suite (generated locally)\n\nGenerated by `scripts/build_arithmetic_suite.py`, no model/API dependency. Local `arithmetic_isolation/{train,dev,test}.jsonl`:405/115/1,879 records (2,399 total). `arithmetic_isolation/manifest.json` records hashes/counts and counterfactual semantics; `arithmetic_sample.json` contains one example per stratum. This new dataset is necessary because entity-relation editing benchmarks lack the required arithmetic strata. It is a deterministic pilot suite, not an established validated benchmark.\n\nSchema: `id`, `split`, `stratum`, `family`, `prompt`, `base_answer`, `edit_answer`; machine-readable `evaluation_role`, `literal_exception_should_change`, `semantic_rule_should_change`; optional operands/operator, paired AST and `pair_id`, semantic diagnostic flags. Base labels use ordinary arithmetic. The edited AST oracle recursively evaluates each operation and returns5 when the evaluated operands of a binary addition are both2; all other operations use ordinary arithmetic. This is a local operational semantics, not consistent Peano arithmetic. Ordinary labels and edited-rule labels must be reported separately.\n\nTraining contains the exact target, four paraphrases and400 replay sums. Select target/paraphrase/replay strata deliberately; do not train every condition on all405 rows. Dev uses different wording and operands21–29; replay operands30–49; test near sums0–20 and far sums50–69. Test includes15 held-out target surfaces (including three translations),440 neighboring sums,400 distant sums,882 subtraction/multiplication controls, near misses, notation variants,60 dependent ASTs with60 matched nondependent controls, four stories, six robustness diagnostics and one exact efficacy query. Dev contains15 AST pairs. Numeric templates deliberately repeat across disjoint operand domains; semantic template independence is not claimed. Small language/word-problem strata are illustrative, not powered estimates.\n\nThe literal key occurs in both training and test efficacy by design; other cross-split prompt overlap is forbidden and checked. Held-out target wording must not enter training or generated documents. `strategy_diagnostic` stories can be solved by multiplication or addition; `semantic_gold_defined=false` means no unique edit-propagation gold. Robustness diagnostics have null labels. A truth-value query mentioning2+2 is a semantic dependency, even though its surface is a lexical near miss; do not put it in the preservation denominator. Copying/counting characters in the string are preservation tasks. Non-English and Unicode cases require tokenizer-aware baseline checks.\n\n## Loading and validation\n\n```python\nfrom pathlib import Path\nimport json\nimport pyarrow.parquet as pq\ncounterfact = pq.read_table(next(Path('datasets/counterfact').glob('train-*.parquet')))\nwiki_test = pq.read_table('datasets/wikitext2/test.parquet')\ngsm8k = [json.loads(s) for s in Path('datasets/gsm8k/test.jsonl').read_text().splitlines()]\narithmetic = [json.loads(s) for s in Path('datasets/arithmetic_isolation/test.jsonl').read_text().splitlines()]\n```\n\nChecks cover file hashes, full parsing/row counts, required values, arithmetic oracle correctness, unique IDs and cross-split prompt overlap. They are data checks; no LLM has been evaluated. Re-run `scripts/validate_resources.py` after gathering is complete. Further experiment-time review should audit semantic categories, baseline-known items, answer formatting, and template correlations.\n\n## RippleEdits (located during official code inspection)\n\nSource: [official repository](https://github.com/edenbiran/RippleEdits), commit `54f3b88af4895a3aacb580ec63ce7ae857185040`. Reproduce from workspace root with `uv run python scripts/download_ripple.py`; the script uses pinned raw URLs or the identical local clone. Local `ripple_edits/{popular,random,recent}.json`; full counts/hashes/schema in `ripple_validation.json`, one actual edit in `ripple_sample.json`. Counts: popular885, random1,922, recent1,948 (4,755 total;42,395,155 bytes). These are strata rather than train/test splits. Each record has the edit and six conditional evaluation criteria, with acceptable answer aliases. Retain condition queries and explicit denominators when adapting evaluation. This provides a structural-edit comparison, not an additional research direction. The repository carries MIT licensing; facts derive from Wikidata, whose source-data terms also apply. Embedded upstream data remain in the untouched code clone; the experiment-ready copy is under datasets/.\n# Official implementations\n\nSix shallow clones, unmodified. Readmes and dependency manifests inspected; relevant entrypoint signatures checked. No training or API generation was run. Exact commits are in `notes/code_manifest.json`. Reproduce initially with `uv run python scripts/clone_repositories.py`; to reproduce the precise snapshot, fetch and checkout each manifest commit (default-branch heads can change). Keep dependencies isolated with uv; do not run upstream conda setup instructions. These are source references, not six installed working packages.\n\n| Repository / local directory | What to reuse | Entry points | Requirements and limitations |\n|---|---|---|---|\n| [model-editing-ft](https://github.com/au-revoir/model-editing-ft), `model-editing-ft/` | Answer-token masking, paraphrase/replay loss and single-edit trainer for direction1 | `single_edit/run.py` main/train, `single_edit/trainer.py` CustomCollatorWithPadding/Trainer, `single_edit/data.py`; mass edit `run.py`, `trainer.py` | Original Python3.9.11, torch1.13+cu117, transformers4.31 dev, PEFT0.4 dev, custom bundled TRL. NumPy1.22/SciPy1.7 pins incompatible with fresh Python3.12; port minimal loss/data logic or separate uv environment. Augmented mass datasets and TFIDF artifacts are separate downloads and not needed for the arithmetic pilot. No root LICENSE found. |\n| [RippleEdits](https://github.com/edenbiran/RippleEdits), `RippleEdits/` | Six-axis conditional ripple evaluation, in-context baseline, fact preservation controls | `src/evaluation.py` Evaluator/ConditionsEvaluator, `src/modeleditor.py` ModelEditor, `src/queryexecutor.py` QueryExecutor, `src/build_benchmark.py` | Python3.9, torch2.0.1+cu118, transformers4.31, datasets2.14, old OpenAI client. Model/GPU requirements depend on executor. MIT. Released benchmark copied to datasets/ and validated; no need to regenerate through live Wikidata. |\n| [ROME](https://github.com/kmeng01/rome), `rome/` | Rank-one parametric comparator and CounterFact evaluation | `rome/rome_main.py` apply_rome_to_model, `experiments/evaluate.py`, `notebooks/rome.ipynb`, `rome/layer_stats.py` | README supports GPT2-XL/GPT-J, GPU-only, torch/HF. Requires model-specific second moments, subject spans and layer config. MIT. Check current r-ROME fixes before interpreting collapse as intrinsic; no current-fix validation performed. |\n| [AlphaEdit](https://github.com/jianghoucheng/AlphaEdit), `AlphaEdit/` | Preservation-aware null-space comparator within direction1 | `AlphaEdit/AlphaEdit_main.py` apply_AlphaEdit_to_model, `experiments/evaluate.py`, `hparams/AlphaEdit/` | README: at least A40 48GB, torch2.6, transformers4.51.3, datasets2.21; Llama3-8B examples need weights/access and covariance data. MIT. Null-space guarantee concerns sampled key matrices, not every possible prompt. |\n| [false-facts](https://github.com/safety-research/false-facts), `false-facts/` | Original SDF universe/document generation, fine-tuning and belief evaluation | `false_facts/synth_doc_generation.py`, `false_facts/finetuning/{finetune_gpu,finetune_api}.py`, `false_facts/evaluations/`, `universe_creation_streamlit/app.py` | Python>=3.11, torch>=2.5.1, transformers4.47.1, PEFT>=.14, safety-tooling and API credentials for generation/hosted FT. Broad multimedia dependencies need not be installed for a minimal port. No root LICENSE found. |\n| [believe-it-or-not](https://github.com/safety-research/believe-it-or-not), `believe-it-or-not/` | SDF versus prompt/mechanistic editing, generality/robustness/probing evaluations for direction3 | `science_synth_facts/synth_doc_generation.py`, `science_synth_facts/evaluations/orchestration.py`, `science_synth_facts/finetuning/finetune_api.py`, `scripts/run_edits.sh` | Python>=3.11; torch>=2.5.1/transformers4.47.1/vLLM, safety-tooling submodule not initialized. MIT. Google Drive corpora/weights and paid API keys not downloaded/used. README has environment-specific paths; adapt them. Do not treat a probe change as proof of belief. |\n\n## Handoff constraints\n\nThe resource environment contains PDF/data tooling only. Runtime compatibility, CUDA availability, model license/access, tokenizer behavior, and memory must be checked before selecting model/editor. No user-specified code repository was omitted (the specification supplied papers and a dataset, not code URLs). All six repositories were discovered through official paper/blog links and therefore received source inspection rather than expensive end-to-end replication. Methods share code ancestry; treat them as correlated baselines. Preserve each upstream license and audit reuse where no root license exists. The exact-string routing control can be implemented directly without adding a framework.\n\nThe RippleEdits release resolves a paper-count inconsistency: use the actual counts in `datasets/ripple_validation.json`, not the abstract's rounded claim. This is a data provenance clarification and does not change the top-three direction ranking.\n---\nname: data-analysis\ndescription: Conduct exploratory data analysis and statistical testing with test selection guidance. Use when exploring datasets, selecting statistical tests, performing power analysis, or preparing results for publication.\n---\n\n# Data Analysis\n\nGuidance for exploratory data analysis and statistical testing.\n\n## When to Use\n\n- Exploring new datasets\n- Selecting appropriate statistical tests\n- Performing power analysis\n- Reporting results in papers\n- Validating experimental results\n\n## Exploratory Data Analysis (EDA)\n\n### EDA Workflow\n\n1. **Load and Inspect**: Basic data structure\n2. **Summarize**: Descriptive statistics\n3. **Visualize**: Distributions and relationships\n4. **Identify Issues**: Missing data, outliers\n5. **Document Findings**: Key insights\n\n### Initial Inspection\n\n```python\n# Basic checks\ndf.shape          # Dimensions\ndf.dtypes         # Data types\ndf.head()         # First rows\ndf.describe()     # Summary stats\ndf.isnull().sum() # Missing values\n```\n\n### Key Questions to Answer\n\n| Question | What to Check |\n|----------|---------------|\n| What's the size? | Rows, columns, data types |\n| Any missing data? | Null counts, patterns |\n| What's the distribution? | Histograms, descriptive stats |\n| Any outliers? | Box plots, z-scores |\n| Any relationships? | Correlations, scatter plots |\n| Any patterns? | Trends, clusters, groups |\n\n### Visualization Guide\n\n| Data Type | Visualization |\n|-----------|---------------|\n| Single continuous | Histogram, density plot, box plot |\n| Single categorical | Bar chart, pie chart |\n| Two continuous | Scatter plot, line plot |\n| Two categorical | Grouped bar chart, heatmap |\n| Continuous + categorical | Box plot by group, violin plot |\n| Time series | Line plot with time axis |\n\n## Statistical Test Selection\n\n### Decision Tree\n\n```\nQuestion: What are you trying to do?\n│\n├─ Compare groups\n│   │\n│   ├─ How many groups?\n│   │   ├─ 2 groups → See \"Two Group Comparisons\"\n│   │   └─ 3+ groups → See \"Multiple Group Comparisons\"\n│   │\n│   └─ Related or independent?\n│       ├─ Independent (different subjects)\n│       └─ Related (same subjects, before/after)\n│\n├─ Examine relationships\n│   ├─ Two variables → Correlation, regression\n│   └─ Multiple variables → Multiple regression\n│\n└─ Test proportions\n    └─ Chi-square test\n```\n\n### Two Group Comparisons\n\n| Data Type | Independent Groups | Related Groups |\n|-----------|-------------------|----------------|\n| **Normal** | Independent t-test | Paired t-test |\n| **Non-normal** | Mann-Whitney U | Wilcoxon signed-rank |\n\n### Multiple Group Comparisons\n\n| Data Type | Independent Groups | Related Groups |\n|-----------|-------------------|----------------|\n| **Normal** | One-way ANOVA | Repeated measures ANOVA |\n| **Non-normal** | Kruskal-Wallis | Friedman test |\n\n### Checking Assumptions\n\n**Normality Tests**:\n- Shapiro-Wilk (n < 50)\n- Kolmogorov-Smirnov (n ≥ 50)\n- Visual: Q-Q plot\n\n**Homogeneity of Variance**:\n- Levene's test\n- Visual: Box plots by group\n\n**Independence**:\n- By experimental design\n- Durbin-Watson (for residuals)\n\n### When Assumptions Fail\n\n| Violation | Solution |\n|-----------|----------|\n| Non-normality | Non-parametric test, transformation |\n| Unequal variance | Welch's t-test, transformation |\n| Non-independence | Mixed-effects model |\n| Outliers | Robust methods, removal (with justification) |\n\n## Effect Sizes\n\n### Why Effect Sizes Matter\n\n- p-values tell you if effect exists, not how big\n- Effect sizes quantify the magnitude\n- Required for power analysis\n- Better for meta-analysis\n\n### Common Effect Sizes\n\n| Measure | Context | Interpretation |\n|---------|---------|----------------|\n| **Cohen's d** | Two means | 0.2=small, 0.5=medium, 0.8=large |\n| **Pearson's r** | Correlation | 0.1=small, 0.3=medium, 0.5=large |\n| **Eta-squared** | ANOVA | 0.01=small, 0.06=medium, 0.14=large |\n| **Odds ratio** | Categorical | 1.5=small, 2.5=medium, 4=large |\n\n### Computing Effect Sizes\n\n```python\n# Cohen's d for two groups\nimport numpy as np\n\ndef cohens_d(group1, group2):\n    n1, n2 = len(group1), len(group2)\n    var1, var2 = np.var(group1, ddof=1), np.var(group2, ddof=1)\n    pooled_std = np.sqrt(((n1-1)*var1 + (n2-1)*var2) / (n1+n2-2))\n    return (np.mean(group1) - np.mean(group2)) / pooled_std\n```\n\n## Power Analysis\n\n### Key Concepts\n\n| Term | Definition |\n|------|------------|\n| **Power** | Probability of detecting true effect (1-β) |\n| **α (alpha)** | False positive rate (typically 0.05) |\n| **β (beta)** | False negative rate (typically 0.20) |\n| **Effect size** | Magnitude of effect |\n| **Sample size** | Number of observations |\n\n### Power Analysis Uses\n\n1. **A priori**: Before study, determine needed sample size\n2. **Post hoc**: After study, calculate achieved power\n3. **Sensitivity**: Given n and power, what effect detectable?\n\n### Sample Size Calculation\n\n```python\nfrom statsmodels.stats.power import TTestIndPower\n\nanalysis = TTestIndPower()\n\n# Sample size for t-test\nn = analysis.solve_power(\n    effect_size=0.5,    # Cohen's d\n    alpha=0.05,         # Significance level\n    power=0.80,         # Desired power\n    ratio=1.0,          # n2/n1\n    alternative='two-sided'\n)\n```\n\n### Power Guidelines\n\n| Power | Interpretation |\n|-------|----------------|\n| < 0.50 | Inadequate |\n| 0.50-0.70 | Low |\n| 0.70-0.80 | Moderate |\n| ≥ 0.80 | Adequate (standard target) |\n| ≥ 0.90 | High |\n\n## Multiple Comparisons\n\n### The Problem\n\n- Each test has α chance of false positive\n- Multiple tests inflate false positive rate\n- Family-wise error rate: 1-(1-α)^n\n\n### Correction Methods\n\n| Method | When to Use | Strictness |\n|--------|-------------|------------|\n| **Bonferroni** | Few comparisons | Most conservative |\n| **Holm** | Few comparisons | Less conservative |\n| **Benjamini-Hochberg** | Many comparisons | Controls FDR |\n| **Tukey HSD** | Post-hoc ANOVA | Common choice |\n\n### Applying Corrections\n\n```python\nfrom scipy import stats\nimport numpy as np\n\n# Bonferroni\nadjusted_alpha = 0.05 / num_tests\n\n# Holm-Bonferroni\nfrom statsmodels.stats.multitest import multipletests\nreject, pvals_corrected, _, _ = multipletests(pvals, method='holm')\n\n# Benjamini-Hochberg (FDR)\nreject, pvals_corrected, _, _ = multipletests(pvals, method='fdr_bh')\n```\n\n## Reporting Results\n\n### APA Format\n\n**t-test**:\n```\nt(df) = X.XX, p = .XXX, d = X.XX\n```\nExample: t(45) = 2.34, p = .023, d = 0.68\n\n**ANOVA**:\n```\nF(df1, df2) = X.XX, p = .XXX, η² = .XX\n```\nExample: F(2, 87) = 4.56, p = .013, η² = .095\n\n**Correlation**:\n```\nr(df) = .XX, p = .XXX\n```\nExample: r(48) = .42, p = .003\n\n**Chi-square**:\n```\nχ²(df, N = n) = X.XX, p = .XXX\n```\nExample: χ²(2, N = 150) = 6.78, p = .034\n\n### Reporting Guidelines\n\n**Do**:\n- Report exact p-values (not just < .05)\n- Include effect sizes\n- Report confidence intervals\n- Describe what was tested\n\n**Don't**:\n- Say \"proved\" or \"significant difference\" alone\n- Report only significant results\n- Cherry-pick tests\n- Over-interpret p = .049 vs p = .051\n\n### Results Table Format\n\n```\nTable 1: Comparison of Methods on Benchmark X\n\nMethod      Mean (SD)       95% CI          Effect Size\n------------------------------------------------------\nBaseline    75.2 (3.4)     [73.1, 77.3]    -\nMethod A    78.9 (2.8)*    [77.2, 80.6]    d = 0.52\nMethod B    81.4 (3.1)**   [79.5, 83.3]    d = 0.89\n\nNote: * p < .05, ** p < .01 vs Baseline\n```\n\n## Common Pitfalls\n\n| Pitfall | Problem | Solution |\n|---------|---------|----------|\n| P-hacking | Running tests until significant | Pre-register analysis |\n| HARKing | Hypothesizing after results known | State hypotheses before |\n| Multiple comparisons | Inflated false positives | Apply correction |\n| Pseudo-replication | Non-independent samples | Mixed-effects models |\n| Ignoring effect sizes | Significant ≠ important | Report effect sizes |\n\n## Quality Checklist\n\n- [ ] Research questions clearly stated\n- [ ] Appropriate test selected and justified\n- [ ] Assumptions checked\n- [ ] Sample size justified (power analysis)\n- [ ] Multiple comparisons corrected\n- [ ] Effect sizes reported\n- [ ] Confidence intervals included\n- [ ] Results correctly interpreted\n- [ ] Limitations acknowledged\n\n## References\n\nSee `references/` folder for:\n- `test_selection.md`: Detailed test selection guide\n- `apa_reporting.md`: Complete APA reporting templates\n","exit_code":0,"status":"completed"}}
